      "notes": "Verdict is coherent and well-structured. The core fiscal logic is sound: scrapping Section 24 is an unfunded tax cut (direction of revenue loss is clear even without OBR costing), and boosting enforcement implies unfunded spending. Both are correctly framed as O12 effects rather than lever judgements. Tier integrity is respected \u2014 the projection about revenue loss is marked 'projected' and the absence of independent costing is honestly flagged. The E41 quote used for claim 3 supports the claim accurately (it confirms no IFS/OBR costing exists). E25 is from The Guardian citing a named academic, which is acceptable as a corroborating source for the measurable enforcement-cuts claim; it is not the sole basis and the claim is appropriately bounded. The 'worsens/minor/low-confidence' verdict is proportionate given genuine magnitude uncertainty. No motive attribution or party-identity language detected. No false balance issue \u2014 direction is not mixed/uncertain, so asymmetry concern does not arise. No lever leakage \u2014 the analysis stays on fiscal impact of the two specific measures. One minor observation: E28 is sourced partly from commonslibrary.parliament.uk and gov.uk but also from totallandlordinsurance.co.uk (commercial/advocacy); the claim it supports is a projected inference rather than a factual assertion, which is appropriate handling."
